# 11. MACHINE LEARNING FEATURES

Implement actual machine learning functionality using Python and Scikit-learn.

Do not create fake ML results or merely display hardcoded predictions.

## A. Automated Expense Categorization

Train a classification model using transaction descriptions and available transaction features.

Requirements:

- Use a suitable text preprocessing pipeline.
- Use TF-IDF for textual features.
- Evaluate models such as Logistic Regression or Random Forest.
- Select a model based on validation performance.
- Save the trained model using Joblib.
- Predict the most likely expense category.
- Return prediction confidence.
- Allow users to correct incorrect predictions.
- Store corrected labels for future retraining.

Do not claim 97%+ accuracy unless it is actually achieved on a properly separated test dataset.

## B. Spending Prediction

Build a spending forecasting model.

Requirements:

- Analyze historical monthly spending.
- Predict expenses for the upcoming month.
- Support category-wise predictions.
- Display predicted spending with confidence or uncertainty ranges where statistically justified.
- Compare predicted expenses with actual expenses.
- Display prediction trends using charts.

Do not produce misleading predictions when insufficient historical data is available.

Clearly communicate when the model requires more transaction history.

## C. Anomaly Detection

Implement an anomaly detection model using Scikit-learn.

Requirements:

- Detect unusually large expenses.
- Detect unusual transaction patterns.
- Identify deviations from normal category spending.
- Support models such as Isolation Forest.
- Generate anomaly scores.
- Display suspicious transactions for user review.

Do not automatically label a transaction as fraudulent. Use language such as "Unusual Spending Detected" or "Needs Review."

